# Commits `git blame` has to skip.
#
# What goes here are the **mechanical changes with no effect**: mass renames,
# reformats, file moves. Without this, `git blame` on any line they touched
# answers "it was the rename", which is true and useless: what one wants to
# know is who wrote the logic.
#
# The criterion for adding one: that its change **cannot alter behaviour**.
# The ones below have it demonstrated, not assumed — the ROMs came out byte
# for byte equal.
#
# A commit that renames *and* fixes something along the way does not go in.
# If needed, it is split into two commits and only the mechanical one is
# ignored.
#
# How to use it:
#
#     git blame --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs <file>
#
# or, so as not to have to remember, once per clone:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs

